  "title": "Bind the criterion to the named testcase in the run's own JUnit report; drop the name-filter option parsing",
  "detail": "The run looped five rounds on one cause. `coveringTestRefusal` accepts file-level evidence: `summary.passed >= 1`, the file in `passedFiles`, and a token heuristic over skip identities. So any option that changes which test runs, such as `-tunrelated` or `'-t' unrelated` in the template, lets an unrelated passing test in the same file satisfy the criterion. Each round rejected one more spelling of the option, and review found the next. Fixing it by parsing shell options can't converge.\n\nFix it at the evidence instead:\n(1) The covering run writes a JUnit report to a fresh, factory-chosen path. Gates already set `LIMITLESS_GATE_REPORT`; add Bun's `--reporter=junit --reporter-outfile=<that path>` as factory-built argv after the template's arguments.\n(2) Parse it with the existing JUnit reader (src/gates/junit.ts).\n(3) Accept only when the report contains a testcase whose file is the requested file and whose full name equals the requested name, with no failure, error or skipped child, and the run left tracked files unchanged.\nIf the report is missing, stale (it existed before the run, or is older than the run's start) or unparseable, refuse. If the template overrides the reporter or the name filter, the named testcase is simply absent and the run is refused: fail closed, with no parsing.\n\nRemove the option-parsing check (~85), `skipMatches`/`matchingSkips` and the summary or passedFiles conditions. Keep passing the name as a literal, regex-escaped filter so the run stays targeted, but correctness must no longer depend on it.\n\nTests, each failing without the change:\n- a template containing `-tunrelated`, `'-t' unrelated` or `--test-name-pattern=unrelated`, with the requested title absent from the file, is refused;\n- the requested title present and passing is accepted;\n- skipped, failed and missing-report cases are refused;\n- a report left over from an earlier run at the same path is refused.\n\nThe issue's budget was about 120 source lines and this PR has about 394. Removing the parsing should bring it down; name any remaining overrun in the report."
 }
